<h1>Data and method</h1>
<p class="lede">How the ranking is counted, what it cannot tell you, and how to query the
full dependency graph &mdash; every declared edge, not just the ranked totals.</p>

<svg class="ornament" width="120" height="16" viewBox="0 0 120 16" fill="none"
    stroke="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
  <path d="M0 8h46" stroke-width="1"/>
  <path d="M52 8c4-6 12-6 16 0-4 6-12 6-16 0z" stroke-width="1"/>
  <path d="M52 8h16" stroke-width="0.6"/>
  <path d="M74 8h46" stroke-width="1"/>
</svg>

<h2>How it is counted</h2>
<ul>
  <li>One release per project &mdash; the highest final release by PEP&nbsp;440 ordering,
      falling back to the highest prerelease for projects that have only prereleases.</li>
  <li>The headline number counts distinct projects declaring an <em>unconditional runtime</em>
      dependency. Dependencies gated behind an <code>extra</code> are counted separately, in
      the &ldquo;incl. extras&rdquo; column. That distinction matters: roughly 37% of all
      declared edges are extras-gated, which is why an unfiltered count puts test tooling
      above numpy.</li>
  <li>Names are normalized per PEP&nbsp;503, so <code>Zope.Interface</code> and
      <code>zope-interface</code> are one project.</li>
  <li>Only projects still live on PyPI are ranked, and only live projects count as
      dependents.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Known limitations</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Yanked releases cannot be excluded. The source table carries no yank status, and
      determining it would take one request per project.</li>
  <li>Only declared dependencies count. Transitive dependencies are not resolved.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Published files</h2>
<p>The full ranked list is published here, rewritten by each monthly run:
<a href="latest.json">latest.json</a> to read, or
<a href="latest.min.json">latest.min.json</a> to fetch. Both hold the same payload.
The complete dependency graph is published as a DuckDB database and a Parquet edge list
on each <a href="https://github.com/miketheman/top-pypi-dependents/releases">release</a>.</p>

<h3>What the JSON looks like</h3>
<p>The shape below is generated from the payload this page was built from, so it cannot
drift from what is served. Only the first row is shown; <code>rows</code> continues for
all {{ "{:,}".format(row_count) }} listed projects.</p>
<pre><code>{{ payload_shape }}</code></pre>
<p><code>previous_rank</code> and <code>rank_change</code> are null for projects that were
not in last month's list. <code>counting.min_dependents</code> is the threshold a project
had to clear to appear at all.</p>

<h3>Querying the graph</h3>
<p>The Parquet edge list carries the liveness of both endpoints, so it can reproduce the
ranking on its own &mdash; no database needed. With the
<a href="https://duckdb.org/docs/installation/">DuckDB CLI</a>:</p>
<pre><code>SELECT dependency, count(DISTINCT dependent) AS dependents
FROM 'edges-{{ asset_month }}.parquet'
WHERE is_runtime AND dependent_is_live AND dependency_is_live
GROUP BY dependency
ORDER BY dependents DESC
LIMIT 10;</code></pre>
<p><strong>Use <code>count(DISTINCT dependent)</code>, not <code>count(*)</code>.</strong>
A project can declare the same dependency more than once under different markers, so
counting rows overcounts &mdash; it puts numpy at 92,112 rather than its actual 90,571
dependents.</p>

<p>The database carries the computed rankings too, alongside the raw edges:</p>
<pre><code>-- What a project declares
SELECT dependency, specifier, extra
FROM dependencies
WHERE dependent = 'jupyterlab' AND is_runtime
ORDER BY dependency;

-- The dependencies most often hidden behind an extra
SELECT dependency, count(*) AS gated
FROM dependencies
WHERE extra IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY dependency
ORDER BY gated DESC
LIMIT 10;</code></pre>
<p>That second query is why the headline column excludes extras: it returns pytest,
pytest-cov, ruff and mypy &mdash; test tooling, not what the ecosystem runs on.</p>
<p>Open the database with
<code>duckdb dependents-{{ asset_month }}.duckdb</code>. Both files hold one snapshot per
monthly run, keyed by <code>snapshot_id</code>.</p>
